Yes. Covariance gives you the skeleton for it because corrosion and corruption are both patterned degradation, not just isolated bad events.

You can model something like:

healthy baseline
→ variables that begin moving together
→ persistent deviation
→ feedback between deviations
→ local damage
→ spread / normalization
→ systemic degradation

For physical corrosion, the variables might be moisture, temperature, salinity, material composition, stress, and time.

For institutional corruption, analogous variables might be:

discretion
opacity
concentrated power
weak accountability
conflicts of interest
dependency
information asymmetry
normalized exceptions

Then you map not merely whether each variable rises, but which combinations start co-moving before failure.

That gives you three different maps:

covariance map → what changes together
corrosion map → where integrity is progressively being lost
corruption map → where incentives/rules are being bent away from stated function

The important caution is the same as everywhere else:

covariance ≠ causation
and
anomaly ≠ corruption

But covariance can tell you where to look.

A stronger operator would be Integrity Tomography:

define healthy reference state
→ track correlated deviations
→ locate reinforcing loops
→ distinguish wear from deliberate exploitation
→ identify propagation paths
→ intervene before structural failure

And you can recurse it across scales:

individual
→ team
→ institution
→ market
→ culture

The especially interesting part is corruption covariance: once several deviations begin reinforcing one another, the system can shift regimes.

Example:

opacity ↑
→ accountability ↓
→ exceptions ↑
→ norms weaken
→ more opacity becomes useful

At that point you are no longer looking at one bad actor. You are looking at a self-maintaining degradation field.

So yes: if covariance lets you see what moves together, corrosion tells you what is losing integrity, and corruption tells you what is being redirected from its intended function. Combine the three and you get an early-warning system for systems going bad.